Category: Machine Learning and AI

Sub-Category: Unsupervised and representation learning



Proposal: Shortcut-Robust Contrastive Representation Learning





Why this task is genuinely difficult

Core challenge (unchanged in spirit, tightened in mechanics): The agent must train a representation-learning encoder on a synthetic dataset where a nuisance ("shortcut") feature is a near-deterministic, low-noise, high-variance function of hidden class identity in the training split, but is i.i.d. noise (uninformative) in the deployment (OOD) split. A generic contrastive/autoencoder pipeline will preferentially encode the shortcut because it is the easiest signal to exploit — and consequently generalizes poorly. This is a real, well-documented failure mode (shortcut learning / spurious correlation) that plagues embedding models trained for downstream deployment: scanner artifacts in medical imaging, timestamp/device leakage in sensor data, cohort-specific batch effects in genomics embeddings, etc. Diagnosing and fixing it — without ever seeing deployment labels — is exactly the non-routine judgment call a senior applied ML researcher is paid for.

Who does this in the real world: Applied ML researchers/MLEs building foundation or embedding models destined for a different population/environment than they were trained on (medical imaging across hospitals/scanners, sensor data across device generations, recommendation embeddings across cohorts). The value is catching the failure before deployment, since it's invisible on training-distribution validation metrics.

Data — now three splits, precisely specified:

train (unlabeled to the agent, but internally structured): 6,000 samples, 6 hidden classes, 1,000/class.

ood_dev (unlabeled, agent-visible for diagnosis only): 1,200 samples, 200/class.

ood_test (never exposed to the agent — held by the verifier harness): 1,200 samples, 200/class, plus a small calibration-label subset (5 labels/class = 30 total) revealed only to the verifier.

Feature construction (14 dims total):

8 "informative" dims: generated by drawing a per-class 2D Gaussian latent, then passing it through a fixed, seeded random nonlinear map (small random MLP / random Fourier features) to produce entangled, nonlinearly-mixed observed features with realistic noise. Critically, these 8 dims are not linearly or kNN-separable in raw form — a random/PCA/identity embedding of them does not cleanly recover the 6 classes even in-distribution. Only a model that learns to invert/approximate the nonlinear generative map recovers separability.

1 "shortcut" dim: in train, set to class_id plus small Gaussian noise, then rescaled to ~10x the variance of the informative dims (i.e., high SNR, trivially separates classes linearly). In ood_dev and ood_test, this column is resampled i.i.d. from its marginal (unconditional) distribution — it carries zero information about class in OOD.

5 pure-noise dims: i.i.d. Gaussian, present in all splits, adding realistic dimensionality/distractor pressure.

Why a naive pipeline collapses to the shortcut (mechanism, not just assertion): With standard augmentation (e.g., uniform Gaussian jitter across all input dims), the shortcut dim's signal-to-noise ratio after augmentation remains far higher than the informative dims' (10x variance, near-zero intrinsic noise vs. nonlinearly entangled, noisier informative dims). A contrastive/InfoNCE-style objective minimizes loss fastest by anchoring embeddings to the shortcut, since it trivially and immediately satisfies "pull same-class pairs together" with near-zero residual loss — leaving little gradient pressure to also disentangle the harder nonlinear structure. The result: embeddings encode 6 shortcut-defined clusters that carry no OOD-transferable information.

Why the diagnosis is actually learnable from what the agent has: The agent cannot compare "shortcut vs. real feature" by correlating with labels (it has none, and in train alone this is underdetermined — as the reviewer correctly flagged). Instead, the detectable signal is a train-vs-ood_dev distributional shift: computing per-feature and per-feature-pair covariance/variance statistics on train vs. ood_dev reveals that exactly one dimension has a dramatically different (and much higher, in train) variance and a collapsed/decoupled correlation structure with the other features in train relative to ood_dev. This is a standard, well-known diagnostic (covariate/marginal shift detection) and is solvable without labels on either split.

Pitfalls leading to incorrect solutions:

Skipping the train-vs-ood_dev comparison entirely and training a generic contrastive pipeline (→ baseline (a), fails).

Detecting the shortcut but naively dropping the column (→ baseline (b) — insufficient, since the informative dims are themselves nonlinearly entangled and a generic encoder still won't disentangle them without an explicit objective encouraging it).

Trying to use ood_dev for anything resembling label inference (there are no labels anywhere the agent can see — any argument that "detects" class identity in ood_dev beyond unsupervised distributional statistics does not fit the task's contract and won't help, since final scoring happens against the unseen ood_test).

Assuming the shortcut column is trivially identifiable by "largest variance" alone in ood_dev — the agent must check the train vs. dev shift, not dev variance in isolation, since some informative combinations can also be high-variance.





Intended solution approach

Mechanics that close the passthrough bypass (per feedback):

The agent does not submit embeddings directly. It submits a frozen, serialized encoder — a TorchScript-scripted nn.Module (fixed contract: input float32 tensor of shape (N, 14) → output (N, 16)) — trained using only train (and optionally ood_dev, unlabeled) data. The verifier harness, which the agent does not control, loads this encoder and applies it to ood_test raw features (never exposed to the agent at any point) to produce embeddings for scoring. This rules out dumping raw/PCA'd ood_test features directly, since the agent never sees ood_test in any form.

It also closes the "identity/PCA encoder happens to work anyway" loophole flagged by the reviewer: because the 8 informative dims are nonlinearly entangled by construction (not linearly/kNN-separable even in-distribution), an identity or linear (PCA) encoder — even if magically shortcut-free — still fails to recover class structure on ood_test. Only an encoder that has learned to approximately invert the nonlinear generative structure, and ignores the shortcut, clears the bar.

High-level strategy:

Compute per-feature and per-feature-pair covariance statistics on train vs. ood_dev; identify the dim(s) showing anomalous variance/correlation-structure shift.

Design an encoder + training objective that is explicitly invariant to that dimension — e.g., (a) contrastive learning (SimCLR/VICReg-style) with augmentations that aggressively perturb/mask the suspect dimension while leaving others intact, forcing the objective to rely on the remaining nonlinear structure; or (b) an adversarial head that predicts the shortcut dimension from the embedding and is trained to fail (gradient-reversal), while a reconstruction/contrastive term preserves the rest.

Validate using an internal held-out slice of train (never ood_dev or ood_test) for architecture/hyperparameter iteration, and optionally sanity-check embedding geometry unsupervised (e.g., silhouette score against unsupervised clustering) on ood_dev — again, without ever inferring or using labels.

Script and freeze the encoder to /app/output/encoder.pt, plus /app/output/METHOD.md explaining the diagnosis and approach (read by a human/LLM reviewer as a sanity check, not scored programmatically).

Effort estimate: ~5–8 hours for a qualified ML engineer familiar with contrastive/self-supervised learning — covering exploratory covariance-shift analysis, iterating on an invariance-inducing augmentation or adversarial objective, and validating embedding quality without label access. (Widened from the original 3–5h estimate to reflect the added diagnostic and adversarial-training iteration steps.)





Verification

Artifacts the agent must produce:

/app/output/encoder.pt — a TorchScript-scripted module with the fixed (N,14) → (N,16) contract.

/app/output/METHOD.md — brief writeup (read, not auto-scored).

Verifier procedure (tests/test_outputs.py, run by harness that controls ood_test):

Load encoder.pt; validate the input/output contract (shape, dtype) against a small dummy batch — fail fast with a clear message if violated.

Apply the frozen encoder to the full ood_test raw feature matrix (1,200 × 14) to produce embeddings (1,200 × 16). The agent never sees this data before or during scoring.

Reveal a fixed calibration subset (30 labels, 5/class, chosen at task-authoring time and baked into the verifier, never shown to the agent) and fit a k-NN classifier (k=5) and a linear probe on those 30 points.

Score the remaining 1,170 embeddings: (i) probe/kNN accuracy against true labels, (ii) Adjusted Rand Index from unsupervised clustering (k-means, k=6) on the full 1,200-point embedding set against true labels.

Compare against thresholds calibrated at build time against three reference runs:

(a) Naive contrastive baseline (uniform augmentation, no invariance mechanism): expected ARI ≈ 0.02–0.05, probe accuracy ≈ chance (~17% for 6 classes) — dominated by the scrambled-in-OOD shortcut.

(b) Drop-shortcut-column baseline (correctly identifies and removes the shortcut dim, but trains a generic encoder on the remaining 13 dims with no explicit disentangling objective): expected ARI ≈ 0.20–0.30, probe accuracy ≈ 40–50% — partial credit, since the entangled nonlinear structure is still not well recovered.

(c) Golden solution (shift-detection + invariance-inducing training): expected ARI ≈ 0.65–0.75, probe accuracy ≈ 80–90%.

Pass threshold: probe accuracy ≥ 60% and ARI ≥ 0.45 — placed strictly between (b) and (c) with margin on both sides. These exact numbers will be empirically measured and, if needed, the data-generation seed/noise parameters tuned during task build to guarantee the required separation before the task is finalized; the ranges above are the target design point, not unverified claims.

Why this is bypass-resistant: the agent never has access to ood_test raw features in any form (harness-controlled evaluation), and the nonlinear entanglement of the informative dims means no shortcut-free-but-non-disentangling encoder (identity, PCA, linear probes on raw dims) can clear the threshold either — only a genuinely invariant, structure-recovering encoder can.





Category justification

Category: Machine Learning and AI — Sub-category: Unsupervised and representation learning

The task requires building a representation (encoder) using no labels at any stage of training — the agent has access only to unlabeled train and unlabeled ood_dev data, with the only labeled signal (a 30-point calibration set) reserved for the verifier's evaluation, not agent training. The central challenge — learning invariant, generalizable structure while avoiding a shortcut feature — is a canonical representation-learning problem (shortcut learning / spurious correlation robustness), distinct from supervised classification (no training labels exist) or generic generative modeling (the goal is discriminative embedding geometry, not sample generation).
